{0 Table of contents}

Insert a table of contents for the current note, and keep it honest: a TOC
that has drifted from the headings it describes is reported as a diagnostic
with a quick fix that rewrites it.

The two halves are one feature because they share one thing — the {e region}:
a delimited span of the document that the server owns. Without a region an
inserted TOC is ordinary prose, indistinguishable from a hand-written list,
and there is nothing to check it against.

{1:region The region}

A TOC region is a {b fenced div} of class [toc] — the vault's own div
extension, the same one [::: warning] uses:

{@markdown[
::: toc
- [Alpha](#alpha)
  - [Beta](#beta)
:::
]}

{2 Why a div and not marker comments}

The other candidate is a pair of HTML comments, which is what [doctoc] and
[markdown-toc] use. The div wins here because this vault {e has} divs:

{ul
  {- {b The body stays Markdown.} A div's content is ordinary block content,
     so the list parses as a list and its links resolve like any other link
     in the vault. A raw [<div>] would not: CommonMark stops parsing Markdown
     inside an HTML block until a blank line, so the body's meaning would
     depend on blank lines the writer has to remember.}
  {- {b It survives rendering as a div.} [::: toc] becomes an element the
     stylesheet can reach, which is what a table of contents wants; an HTML
     comment renders to nothing at all.}
  {- {b It is syntax the vault already has.} A region is a block the parser
     understands, not a convention layered on top of comments it ignores.}
}

The cost, stated plainly: a div is visible in the source and in the output,
where a comment is invisible in both. That is the trade taken.

{2:recognition Recognition is the parser's}

A region is a {!Cmarkit.Block.Ext_div} whose class is exactly [toc], found by
folding the parsed document. There is no second reading of the text, and no
second definition of what a fence is: the same AST that supplies the headings
supplies the divs.

This is a requirement, not an implementation detail. Recognizing fences by
matching lines would be a second implementation of div syntax, free to drift
from the one that decides what the note actually is — and it does drift
immediately: a [::: toc] inside a fenced code block is not a div, a nested
[::: toc] is closed before its parent, and both an indented fence and one
inside a list item are divs that a naive line scan would misplace.

Two consequences worth stating:

{ul
  {- The document is parsed with layout on
     ([Oystermark.Parse.of_string ~layout:true]). Without it the div is still
     a div, but {!Cmarkit.Block.Div.opening_fence} and
     {!Cmarkit.Block.Div.closing_fence} carry no location, and the feature
     needs the {e delimiters}, not just the content.}
  {- There is no {e stray closing fence} case. A lone [:::] closes some other
     div — [::: warning] and its neighbours — and never reaches this feature.
     That is the concrete advantage of a class-bearing opening fence over a
     symmetric comment pair.}
}

{2 Malformed and nested regions}

{ul
  {- A [::: toc] the parser closed at the end of the document rather than with
     a fence — {!Cmarkit.Block.Div.closing_fence} is [None] — is
     {b unterminated}. It swallows the rest of the note, which is not a span
     to rewrite silently, so no TOC is checked or updated there and it is
     reported (see {!diagnostics}).}
  {- A [::: toc] inside another one is body text, not a region of its own:
     the fold stops at the outer div. Note what the parser does with
     [::: toc] / [::: toc] / [:::]: the lone fence closes the {e inner} div,
     leaving the outer unterminated. A line-based scan would have paired the
     outer fence with that [:::] and rewritten the wrong span.}
  {- A document may hold several regions. Each is checked and updated
     independently, and each holds the {e same} generated TOC — the content is
     a function of the document's headings, not of where the region sits.}
}

{1:generation Generated content}

The TOC is generated from the headings of the {e freshly parsed buffer} —
the same source the link diagnostics use, not the vault index, so that it
tracks a heading the moment it is typed. See {!page-"feature-document-sync"}.

Each heading becomes one list item:

{@markdown[
- [heading text](#slug)
]}

{ul
  {- The link text is the heading's plain text, as
     {!Oystermark.Vault.Index.Entry.headings} reports it, with [\[], [\]]
     and the backslash itself backslash-escaped, so that a heading containing
     brackets still produces a well-formed link.}
  {- The destination is the heading's {e slug} — the identifier the parser
     assigned, including its [-1], [-2] deduplication and any explicit
     [ {#id} ] the author wrote above the heading. A slug is therefore unique
     within the file, and every generated link resolves: intra-note fragment
     links reach all three anchor kinds (see
     {!page-"feature-attribute-anchors"}).}
  {- Indentation is two spaces per level {e below the shallowest heading
     present}, so a note whose headings start at [##] is not indented as
     though an absent [#] were there. Levels skipped in the document are
     skipped in the indentation too: [##] followed by [####] indents by one
     step, not two.}
  {- A heading whose text is empty is omitted: it has nothing to click.}
  {- Headings that fall {e inside} a TOC region are omitted, so a TOC that
     happens to sit under a heading is never described by itself.}
}

The links are generated, and the features that count links know it: a TOC
entry is not a reference to the heading it names. See
{!page-"feature-codelens-reference-counts".self} and
{!page-"feature-find-references".generated}.

A document with no headings generates an empty body — the two fences with
nothing between them. This is a legitimate up-to-date state, not an error;
the region is a placeholder that fills in as the note grows.

{1:staleness Staleness}

A region is {b stale} when its body differs from the generated content, after
normalizing both:

{ol
  {- trailing whitespace is stripped from every line;}
  {- leading and trailing blank lines are dropped.}
}

Normalization is deliberately narrow. Reordered items, an edited link text, a
removed entry and a heading added since the TOC was written all count as
differences — that is the point. Only whitespace a formatter might have
touched is forgiven.

{1:diagnostics Diagnostics}

Published with the rest of the document's diagnostics, on the same cadence
(see {!page-"feature-diagnostics"}), with [source] ["oystermark"] and
severity [Warning].

{t
  | Condition | Range | Message |
  |-----------|-------|---------|
  | stale region | the opening fence line | ["table of contents is out of date"] |
  | unterminated opening fence | that line | ["unterminated table of contents: missing a closing ::: fence"] |
}

An up-to-date region produces nothing. A document with no [::: toc] produces
nothing — the feature is inert until the writer asks for it, and a div of any
other class is not its business.

{1:actions Code actions}

{2 Insert}

Offered as a [Refactor] when the cursor is {e not} inside a TOC region,
titled [Insert table of contents]. Its edit inserts, at the start of the
cursor's line, the opening fence, the generated body, and the closing fence,
each on its own line. The line the cursor was on follows the closing fence
unchanged; no blank line is added, because a fence line already ends its own
block.

It is offered even in a note that already has a region elsewhere — a note may
want a second one — and even when the note has no headings, which inserts an
empty region.

{2 Update}

Offered as a [QuickFix], marked preferred, when the requested range
intersects a {e stale} region: [Update table of contents]. Its edit replaces
the region's body — the span between the two fence lines, fences excluded —
with the generated content. The fences themselves are never rewritten, so an
update cannot lose the region.

A region that is already up to date offers nothing: there is no edit to make,
and an action that does nothing is noise in the menu.

{1:frame Which content is edited}

Both the diagnostic and the update act on the {e buffer}, not on disk. This
is not the usual choice for a code action (see
{!page-"feature-document-sync".mixed_frame}), and here it is the necessary
one: a TOC's whole job is to describe headings that were just typed, and an
update computed against disk content would replace a span of the buffer with
text derived from a different version of the file.

{1 Testing obligations}

{ul
  {- Finding regions: well-formed, unterminated, a div of another class, a
     nested [::: toc], a bare [:::] inside a region, several regions in one
     document, an indented fence, and a fence inside a code block (which is
     {e not} a region — the case that pins recognition to the parser).}
  {- Generation: nesting, skipped levels, a document starting at [##], an
     explicit [ {#id} ] anchor, duplicate heading texts (deduped slugs), a
     heading containing brackets, an empty heading, no headings at all.}
  {- Staleness: identical body, whitespace-only difference (fresh), added
     heading, removed heading, reordered items, edited link text.}
  {- Server level: the diagnostic appears on the opening fence line; the quick fix
     replaces the body and yields the generated TOC when applied; the insert
     action is absent inside a region and present outside it.}
}
